<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Travunia</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1186 CE (approx.) · capital Trebinje</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Slavic principality around Trebinje in eastern Herzegovina, ruled by local lords under Serbian, Byzantine and Dukljan overlords.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Travunia formed in the 7th to 9th centuries among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> settled in the hinterland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> records that its ruler Krajina, son of Beloje, married the daughter of the Serbian prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlastimir">Vlastimir</a> in the 9th century, tying it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-serbia-early-medieval">Principality of Serbia (early medieval)</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ruled by hereditary župans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebinje">Trebinje</a>, it had towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/risan">Risan</a> and controlled routes from the coast into the interior. It was usually dependent on a larger neighbour: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, and from the 11th century <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a>, whose rulers often installed relatives there.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-zupan">Grand Župan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-serbia">Grand Principality of Serbia</a> annexed Travunia, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a>, around 1186. It remained part of the Serbian lands, later passing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, and its centre <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebinje">Trebinje</a> is now in Bosnia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/travunia.jpg" alt="Travunia" width="320" height="223"><figcaption>Image: User:PANONIAN, derivative work: User:Ceha · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paganija,_Zahumlje,_Travunija,_Duklja,_Croatian_view.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1186 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Trebinje</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Krajina Belojević</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Serbian (Slavonic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Bosnia and Herzegovina, Montenegro, Croatia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Terbounia, Tribunia</dd></dl></aside></div>
